WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.000
MODOK is possibly the strangest character
ever created by the folks at Marvel.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.310
An evil giant head with arms and legs, MODOK
is bent on taking over the world, and he's

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.240
also incredibly goofy.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.210
So will we ever see him battling the Avengers
in a Marvel movie?

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.370
Well, let's find out.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:21.670
Originally created by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby,
MODOK first appeared in 1967's Tales of Suspense

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.880
#94 as a foe for Captain America.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.869
At his core, he's exactly what his name says
he is - a Mobile Organism Designed Only for

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:29.960
Killing.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.579
As for his origin, George Tarleton had signed
up to work for the acronym-loving science-terrorists

00:00:34.579 --> 00:00:39.120
of Advanced Idea Mechanics, only to find that
he was the dumbest guy in the room.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.530
Admittedly, there's a significant chance of
that happening when the room is otherwise

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:46.870
full of mad scientists, but George had the
opportunity to get a little smarter by letting

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.500
AIM experiment on him.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.320
The original intent was to turn him into MODOC,
with a C instead of a K - the Mobile Organism

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.420
Designed Only for Computing, whose genetically
augmented brain could help AIM with data processing.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.440
Unfortunately, he became way more aggressive
than they'd anticipated, which earned him

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.299
a more accurate acronym, and took the position
of Scientist Supreme.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:10.539
In the process, George was physically transformed,
with his head growing so massive that his

00:01:10.539 --> 00:01:14.689
body could no longer support it, requiring
a floating rocket chair to move.

00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:17.610
The trade-off, though, came in the form of
mental super powers.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.439
“MODOC will have power beyond anything man
has ever seen.

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:24.280
His very mind is a weapon!"

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.389
Over the next few decades, MODOK would be
a perennial foe in the Marvel Universe, with

00:01:28.389 --> 00:01:33.320
AIM becoming a sort of utility player supervillain
organization that could reasonably go up against

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.780
almost any hero.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.959
A group that's entirely devoted to mad science
for the sake of mad science, directed by the

00:01:38.959 --> 00:01:43.069
incomprehensible whims of a giant floating
head, can show up pretty much anywhere.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:47.380
Through it all, MODOK was at the center of
AIM's advanced ideological machinations.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.599
"MODOC CONTROLS AIM.

00:01:49.599 --> 00:01:51.810
NOW AND FOREVER.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:57.349
A few of his most memorable exploits during
this period involved creating a giant robot

00:01:57.349 --> 00:02:01.679
body that was properly proportionate to his
own giant head so that he could fight the

00:02:01.679 --> 00:02:05.380
Hulk, a plan with so many problems that you
really start to understand why "computing"

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:06.679
wasn't really his forte.

00:02:06.679 --> 00:02:10.929
He also once teamed up with the Headmen, a
group of D-list villains united by the fact

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:14.469
that they all had weird heads, in an attempt
to take over the world.

00:02:14.469 --> 00:02:18.040
That's the kind of story that MODOK was winding
up in, and it would only get weirder from

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.040
there.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.129
The life of a MODOK is a lonely one.

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:24.069
When one is designed only for killing, can
they ever truly know ... love?

00:02:24.069 --> 00:02:29.159
If MODOK's past is anything to go by, the
answer is a resounding "no," but that's never

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:33.290
stopped him from trying to create some kind
of Mutually Attracted Technological Entity

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.670
to ease those lonely nights of plotting world
domination.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.019
The first attempt at a M.A.T.E. came when
S.H.I.E.L.D. scientist Kate Waynesboro was

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:43.530
transformed into the imaginatively named "Ms.
MODOK."

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:47.140
She didn't last long, turning against her
creators and eventually stumbling into some

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:51.310
kind of reverse-MODOK-ifying machine that
undid her transformation.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:56.400
The second was slightly more successful, by
a set of very specific standards for "success."

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.450
A woman who was originally thought to be Hank
Pym's dead first wife, Maria, underwent a

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.879
similar procedure to George Tarleton.

00:03:02.879 --> 00:03:06.840
The difference was that it only caused her
brain to balloon to the size of a small zeppelin

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.610
and not the rest of her head.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:12.800
Since she wasn't "Mobile," she was initially
designated a Specialized Organism Designed

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.060
for Aggressive Maneuvers or SODAM.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.450
Eventually, though, she finished a complete
MODOKification, and having been granted her

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:21.680
own rocket chair, she became MODAM.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.670
Perhaps surprisingly, MODAM and MODOK never
really hit it off.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:29.689
As you might be able to tell already, MODOK
found his way into some of the Marvel Universe's

00:03:29.689 --> 00:03:30.689
weirder stories.

00:03:30.689 --> 00:03:35.080
In fact, he would eventually come to be a
symbol of the weirder side of Marvel himself.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:39.290
Maybe it was the purple and orange design
that made MODOK look like a malevolent Easter

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:40.290
egg.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.379
Maybe it was the innate hilarity of the phrase
"designed only for killing."

00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:46.969
Maybe it was that he sometimes seemed like
the one villain who was allowed to be incredibly

00:03:46.969 --> 00:03:50.379
silly while still being presented as a deadly
mastermind.

00:03:50.379 --> 00:03:54.629
Whatever the reason, he went far beyond your
average weirdo villain and became a true cult

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:59.420
favorite, inspiring fanzines like the pseudo-scholarly
Journal of MODOK Studies and occasionally

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.529
wrangling his way into a starring role.

00:04:01.529 --> 00:04:05.510
With all that in mind, what are the chances
that he'll show up in a Marvel movie someday?

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:08.400
Well, they're probably better than you think

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.599
MODOK has never really been the go-to villain
outside of the comics, but that hasn't stopped

00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:16.739
him from making a handful of appearances in
slightly more mainstream media.

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:21.329
He's appeared on a lot of the Marvel animated
shows, albeit with some notable changes, starting

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:25.819
back in the '90s with the Iron Man cartoon
that made up half of The Marvel Action Hour.

00:04:25.819 --> 00:04:29.669
Aside from changing the "K" in his name to
stand for "Konquest," the big difference there

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:30.880
was in size.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.370
Rather than just being a gigantic head perched
precariously on top of a comparatively tiny

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:40.020
body, he was shrunk down, with the end result
being that he was about as big as your standard-issue

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.460
toddler with a head the size of a basketball.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:45.870
Why they changed it remains a mystery, but
given that the shows of the era were heavily

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:50.190
tied into toy lines, it might've been that
a slightly smaller-sized MODOK was easier

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:54.759
to make as an action figure that could fit
on the same size card as the rest of the line.

00:04:54.759 --> 00:04:58.620
Regardless of the reasons, it gave us the
very memorable scene of a genocidal supervillain

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.860
going undercover and being pushed around in
a baby carriage.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.180
"It's as you feared.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:05.340
They know."

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.669
"I fear nothing, Hypnosha!"

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:13.250
Beyond that, MODOK's most surprising appearance
probably came in the world of video games,

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:17.880
when he was added to the roster of Marvel
vs. Capcom 3: Fate of Two Worlds as a playable

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:18.930
character.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:23.090
You might think that being a giant, easily
targeted head with comparably tiny arms and

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:27.639
legs would put him at a distinct disadvantage
in a game about punching people in the face,

00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:31.000
but it seems like he's a Mental Organism Designed
Only for K.O.s

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.070
Out of all MODOK's various media appearances,
the strangest might be the one where we find

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.970
out he already technically exists in the MCU.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:43.819
Okay, when we say that MODOK is technically
already a part of the MCU, the word "technically"

00:05:43.819 --> 00:05:45.889
is doing a significant amount of heavy lifting.

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:50.660
He has indeed made an appearance in an official
installment of the MCU, but it's not one that

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:52.789
most moviegoers would know about.

00:05:52.789 --> 00:05:55.150
He's in Iron Man 3: The Official Game.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:59.639
To be fair, the video games are dubious at
best when it comes to tying into the actual

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:00.720
MCU.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.940
After all, the games aren't so much "contradicted"
by the movies as they are completely ignored

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:09.080
by them, but they do give a few underrated
characters a chance to get a little closer

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.430
to the MCU than they would've otherwise.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:16.470
The Captain America tie-in games, for instance,
have some really great deep cuts, like Diamondback

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.240
and the militant anarchist forces of U.L.T.I.M.A.T.U.M.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:23.669
As for MODOK, the version that shows up in
the game has an interesting twist to him.

00:06:23.669 --> 00:06:28.120
Instead of George Tarleton, this version of
MODOK is Aldrich Killian, the mastermind behind

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.539
Extremis who dies at the end of the movie.

00:06:30.539 --> 00:06:34.690
Apparently, he had a contingency plan, which
involved backing up his brain and then becoming

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:36.729
a MODOK to get his revenge.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:40.860
However, the biggest factor working against
MODOK's chances for showing up in a film is

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:43.490
that he's actually getting his own TV show.

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:47.960
It's an animated sitcom starring Patton Oswalt
in the title role, and it will focus on MODOK's

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:51.169
home life with his wife and kids as he faces
a midlife crisis.

00:06:51.169 --> 00:06:52.630
No, really.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:53.630
That's happening.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:55.789
It'll be on Hulu later in 2020.

00:06:55.789 --> 00:06:59.900
The setup of the show is that after failing
to conquer the world despite many, many attempts,

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.340
MODOK has lost control of AIM.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.340
Facing a forced early retirement, he now finds
himself without a purpose in life, because,

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.380
you know, he's literally designed to do one
thing.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:13.700
In addition to Oswalt, the show's cast should
be pretty familiar to fans of TV comedy.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:18.099
Aimee Garcia, whose previous comic book-themed
credits include playing crime scene investigator

00:07:18.099 --> 00:07:22.650
Ella Lopez on Lucifer, will be playing MODOK's
wife, Jodie, who runs a mommy blog.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:27.470
Their two kids, high school senior Melissa
and tween Lou, will be voiced by Melissa Fumero

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:32.849
of Brooklyn 99 and Ben Schwartz, who may be
best known for his role on Parks &amp; Recreation.

00:07:32.849 --> 00:07:37.509
Originally, MODOK was set to be part of its
own bizarre shared universe of animated shows,

00:07:37.509 --> 00:07:41.520
alongside fellow Marvel Universe oddballs
Hit-Monkey, Howard the Duck, and Tigra and

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.000
Dazzler as "The Offenders."

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.580
That deal wound up falling through, but MODOK
will continue on regardless.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:51.830
All right, let's say that Marvel Studios does
in fact want to work MODOK into their universe,

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:53.780
but they don't want to use him as a villain.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:57.389
Maybe they're worried that having a bad guy
with a giant head fighting a superhero was

00:07:57.389 --> 00:08:01.490
the one, singular fatal flaw that turned audiences
off of the Green Lantern movie.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.190
So how do they bring him in?

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:05.789
As always, the comics have provided an answer.

00:08:05.789 --> 00:08:10.069
As the success of Suicide Squad proved, audiences
can get behind a heist story about a bunch

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:11.610
of misfit supervillains.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:15.930
If they're looking to do their own villain-centric
action-comedy romp, then the MCU movies could

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:20.040
do a lot worse than lifting the plot of Super-Villain
Team-Up: MODOK's 11.

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:24.169
As you might expect from the title, MODOK's
11 involves MODOK putting together a team

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:28.310
of C-list villains for a job that involves
stealing a massive, potentially catastrophic

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.469
power source called the "hypernova."

00:08:30.469 --> 00:08:34.870
None of the other characters involved have
been in the films, but the basic idea of reuniting

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:38.650
the MCU's surviving villains under MODOK's
commanding cranium could make for a super

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:39.650
fun movie.

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:43.700
It could be the perfect vehicle to bring back
minor characters like Batroc or the baddies

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:47.720
who've been on ice due to a lack of sequels,
like the Abomination.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:51.890
Let's say Marvel Studios wants to go a different
way and fully embrace all the weirdness that

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:55.330
comes along with a rocket-propelled giant
head that wants to take over the world with

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:56.810
his brain powers.

00:08:56.810 --> 00:09:00.480
There's a comic they could draw on from that,
too, and it might just be the single greatest

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.590
MODOK appearance of all time.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:07.030
It happened in the pages of the kid-friendly
Marvel Adventures: The Avengers #9.

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:08.640
The premise of the story is simple.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.750
When the Avengers raid AIM's headquarters,
MODOK uses his brain powers to lure them into

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:15.610
a chamber that turns them all into MODOKs.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:19.330
The problem is that they're still the Avengers,
so they break out in their own rocket-powered

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:22.120
hoverchairs and continue doing Avenger things.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:26.150
The funniest part is that while they carry
on being superheroes, the public is completely

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:30.900
weirded out by it, and everyone reacts with
less cheering and more with just quietly being

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:31.900
disturbed.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:36.040
Eventually, Wolverine's mutant power counteracts
the MODOK transformation, and he's able to

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:39.320
reverse it for the rest of the team before
they decide to take over the world to run

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:40.730
it more efficiently.

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:41.730
Let's be honest.

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:45.770
This doesn't seem likely to make it into a
live-action MCU project, but consider the

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:46.770
following.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:50.570
Given how eager they were to finally move
on from their rigorous, time-consuming Marvel

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:54.710
roles, having Chris Evans and Robert Downey
Jr. come back to voice computer-animated MODOK

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:59.380
versions of themselves might be the only possible
way to get those guys back for another MCU

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:00.380
appearance.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:01.380
"Whatever it takes."

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:02.580
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:06.760
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
comic characters are coming soon.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:09.600
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

